{"combines": true, "proposition": "XPS (Co 2p Co3+ deconvolved component) and EELS (Co L3 edge position) are two independent spectroscopic probes of Co oxidation/valence state, and both show the identical sample grouping: Co5Fe5 and Co5Fe3Cr2 elevated (larger Co3+ component; Co L3 edge blue-shifted by 1.35 eV) relative to Co and Co5Cr5. Because a surface-sensitive core-level technique (XPS) and a separate edge-spectroscopy technique (EELS) independently converge on the same grouping, together they corroborate that the elevated Co valence in the Fe-containing samples is a real chemical/electronic-structure difference rather than an artifact specific to one measurement (e.g., XPS peak-fitting/charging or EELS edge-fitting idiosyncrasies).", "limits": ["Neither observation alone, nor the two together, establishes the mechanism by which Fe incorporation raises Co valence -- only that the two spectroscopic signatures move together across the same four samples.", "The two quantities are not commensurate: the XPS observation is a deconvolved compositional fraction (relative amount of the Co3+ component) while the EELS observation is a single energy shift (1.35 eV); the pairing cannot be combined into one unified magnitude of valence change, only a consistent qualitative direction across the same sample grouping.", "The comparison spans only four samples (Co, Co5Cr5, Co5Fe5, Co5Fe3Cr2), too few points to support a dose-response or quantitative relationship between Fe content and valence shift -- only the grouping (Fe-containing vs not) is supported.", "Co5Fe3Cr2 contains both Fe and Cr, so this sample alone cannot separate a Fe effect from a Fe-Cr interaction; identification of Fe as sufficient rests on Co5Fe5 (Fe only, effect present) and Co5Cr5 (Cr only, effect absent), which is already visible from either technique's own four-sample panel and is not something the XPS-EELS pairing itself adds."], "not_identifiable": null, "quantity": {"a": {"kind": "amount, normalized/fractional", "what": "deconvolved Co3+ component of the Co 2p XPS signal (relative fraction of total Co 2p), compared across Co, Co5Cr5, Co5Fe5, Co5Fe3Cr2"}, "b": {"kind": "shift (peak-position energy shift)", "what": "Co L3 edge position in EELS, blue-shifted by 1.35 eV in Co5Fe3Cr2 and Co5Fe5 relative to Co and Co5Cr5"}}, "labels": {"dependency": "real", "inference_validity": "follows, weakly", "causal_strength": "discriminating"}}